@page "/pia"

<PageTitle>Portable Identity Assurance</PageTitle>

<div class="page-header">
    <FluentLabel Typo="Typography.PageTitle">Portable Identity Assurance in Healthcare</FluentLabel>
    <div class="page-subtitle">
        A worked, runnable build of the architecture in
        <strong>"Portable Identity Assurance in Healthcare: A Framework for Minor Identity and a Foundation for
        Federated Trust"</strong> — issue, hold, present, and verify durable health-identity credentials across
        organizational boundaries using SD-JWT VC + OID4VC.
        <br />
        <span class="muted">
            Source: Max Templeton, Cambia Health Solutions (DRAFT v0.9) —
            <a href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/11P-XSaal9cgpXTzp8W5Jc50ppZoeN7PF/edit" target="_blank" rel="noopener">document</a>.
            Summaries here are our own analysis; we do not reproduce the paper's text.
        </span>
    </div>
</div>

<FluentCard Style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
    <h3>The four-tier model</h3>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Tier 1 — Delegate identity verification.</strong> A responsible adult is identity-proofed and mapped to an assurance level (FAST IDIAL).</li>
        <li><strong>Tier 2 — Delegated proofing &amp; issuance.</strong> The adult vouches for the minor; an SD-JWT VC is issued via OID4VCI carrying the (discounted) assurance level.</li>
        <li><strong>Tier 3 — Presentation &amp; relying-party decision.</strong> The wallet presents only the needed claims via OID4VP; the verifier weighs assurance against transaction risk.</li>
        <li><strong>Tier 4 — Governance / trust framework.</strong> Who is authoritative for what — the trust list the verifier consults.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="muted">
        Two foundations cut across every tier: <strong>SD-JWT selective disclosure</strong> (the credential format) and
        <strong>delegated proofing + assurance levels</strong> (the trust basis). We build those first, then layer the
        issuance pathways, custody lifecycle, verification, and governance on top.
    </p>
</FluentCard>

<FluentCard Style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
    <h3>Runnable services (the real multi-party topology)</h3>
    <p class="muted">
        Each paper actor is its own running app behind the proxy, so the
        <a href="/live-flow">Live Flow</a> captures genuine cross-service traffic. Open any service:
    </p>
    <ul class="pia-services">
        <li><a href="/identity-csp/" target="_blank">Identity-CSP</a> — delegated proofing → FAST IDIAL</li>
        <li><a href="/payer-issuer/" target="_blank">Payer-Issuer</a> — Pathway A (enrollment)</li>
        <li><a href="/provider-issuer/" target="_blank">Provider-Issuer</a> — Pathway B (in-person)</li>
        <li><a href="/csp-issuer/" target="_blank">CSP-Issuer</a> — Pathway C (consults the Identity-CSP)</li>
        <li><a href="/holder/" target="_blank">Guardian Wallet</a> — holds the minor's VC; can transfer custody</li>
        <li><a href="/holder-minor/" target="_blank">Minor's Wallet</a> — receives custody via re-issuance</li>
        <li><a href="/verifier/" target="_blank">Verifier</a> — OID4VP / SD-JWT relying party</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="muted">
        See it animate end to end on <a href="/live-flow">Live Flow</a> →
        <strong>Run Portable Identity Assurance (CSP-backed)</strong>: delegated proofing → CSP-backed
        issuance → guardian holds → presents only <code>age_over_18</code>.
    </p>
</FluentCard>

